<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Organization.</span> In the next section, we present constructions of very dense sets without <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-term arithmetic progressions in both the interval and cyclic setting. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Lovász local lemma consequences ‣ Three-color van der Waerden numbers grow super-exponentially" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we present a simple consequence of the Lovász local lemma that shows there are set colorings which are far from containing monochromatic <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-term arithmetic progressions. In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Random Shifted Product Constructions ‣ Three-color van der Waerden numbers grow super-exponentially" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we provide several different variants of our random shifted product construction. In Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Lower bounds on van der Waerden numbers ‣ Three-color van der Waerden numbers grow super-exponentially" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we combine the various tools from the previous sections to obtain Theorems <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Three-color van der Waerden numbers grow super-exponentially" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Three-color van der Waerden numbers grow super-exponentially" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> giving lower bounds on van der Waerden numbers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">All logarithms are base <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> unless otherwise specified. We also sometimes omit floor and ceiling signs when this has no effect on the argument.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Disclosure.</span> The authors used Chat GPT Pro 5.5 for copyediting assistance during the final edits of this paper. All mathematical arguments and results in this paper were human-deduced.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Very dense sets without long arithmetic progressions</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Erdős and Turán <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> gave the following simple construction of a set of nonnegative integers with no long arithmetic progressions. For a prime <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of nonnegative integers which, when written in base <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, has no digit equal to <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p-1</annotation></semantics></math>. The set <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> has no <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-term arithmetic progression. Indeed, if otherwise, letting <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,a+d,\ldots,a+(p-1)d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,a+d,\ldots,a+(p-1)d</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-term arithmetic progression with elements in <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the position <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> of the least nonzero digit in base <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> of the common difference <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. As we go through the elements of the arithmetic progression, we get every element of <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1,\ldots,p-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1,\ldots,p-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> appearing exactly once in position <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in base <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. One can also check that <math id="S2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely the greedy construction of a set of nonnegative integers which has no <math id="S2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-term arithmetic progression, so it is natural to suspect that it is rather dense (or at least at the beginning). Note that <math id="S2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> contains exactly <math id="S2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p-1)^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p-1)^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> elements from <math id="S2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1,\ldots,p^{t}-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1,\ldots,p^{t}-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the set of nonnegative integers with at most <math id="S2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> digits when written in base <math id="S2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, if <math id="S2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=p^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=p^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|T_{p}\cap\{0,1,\ldots,N-1\}|/N=(1-1/p)^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|T_{p}\cap\{0,1,\ldots,N-1\}|/N=(1-1/p)^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. More generally, it follows from a simple induction argument on <math id="S2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> that if a positive integer <math id="S2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is such that <math id="S2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N-1</annotation></semantics></math> has at most <math id="S2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> digits when written in base <math id="S2.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|T_{p}\cap\{0,1,\ldots,N-1\}|/N\geq(1-1/p)^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|T_{p}\cap\{0,1,\ldots,N-1\}|/N\geq(1-1/p)^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The function <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{k}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{k}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum size of a subset of <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[N]</annotation></semantics></math> which contains no <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-term arithmetic progression. Szemerédi’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> states that for every fixed <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{k}(N)=o(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{k}(N)=o(N)</annotation></semantics></math>. From the above discussion, we have the following corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmresult1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmresult1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmresult1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmresult1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmresult1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmresult1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.Thmresult1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is prime, <math id="S2.Thmresult1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive integer, and <math id="S2.Thmresult1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\leq p^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\leq p^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.Thmresult1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{p}(N)\geq(1-1/p)^{t}N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{p}(N)\geq(1-1/p)^{t}N</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\Theta(\log N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\Theta(\log N)</annotation></semantics></math> we get <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{k}(N)\geq(1-\Theta(1/\log N))N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{k}(N)\geq(1-\Theta(1/\log N))N</annotation></semantics></math> from Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmresult1" title="Corollary 2.1. ‣ 2 Very dense sets without long arithmetic progressions ‣ Three-color van der Waerden numbers grow super-exponentially" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> and Bertrand’s postulate. In particular, in this case there are subsets of <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[N]</annotation></semantics></math> of density <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-o(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-o(1)</annotation></semantics></math> that do not contain a <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-term arithmetic progression. Such sets are a useful starting point for randomized shifted product constructions. However, for technical reasons, it is much more convenient to transition from the setting of discrete intervals to cyclic groups, so let us introduce some more notation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">All groups we consider here are abelian. In an abelian group, a <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-term arithmetic progression (<math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-AP for short) is a sequence <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,a+d,a+2d,\ldots,a+(k-1)d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,a+d,a+2d,\ldots,a+(k-1)d</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> nonzero. We say a subset of the group is <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-AP-free</span> if it does not contain a <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-AP. The following is an analogue of the Erdős-Turán construction in certain cyclic groups.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmresult2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmresult2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmresult2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmresult2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmresult2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmresult2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider primes <math id="S2.Thmresult2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},\ldots,p_{s}\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},\ldots,p_{s}\leq k</annotation></semantics></math> (not necessarily distinct). Let <math id="S2.Thmresult2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\prod_{i=1}^{s}p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\prod_{i=1}^{s}p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmresult2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\min_{i\in[s]}p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\min_{i\in[s]}p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is a subset <math id="S2.Thmresult2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{Z}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{Z}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S2.Thmresult2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|=\prod_{i=1}^{s}(p_{i}-1)\geq(1-1/m)^{s}N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|=\prod_{i=1}^{s}(p_{i}-1)\geq(1-1/m)^{s}N</annotation></semantics></math> that is <math id="S2.Thmresult2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-AP-free.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_itemize">
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We can write <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> as a product of cyclic groups, each of prime power order with distinct primes, and these primes are each at most <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that it suffices to prove the lemma in the case <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is such a prime power, as we can get the desired set <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> in the general case by taking the product of the sets we get in the prime power case. So suppose <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=p^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=p^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> prime, <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> a positive integer. We view the elements of <math id="S2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\ldots,N-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\ldots,N-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{Z}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{Z}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> consist of those elements that have no digit equal to <math id="S2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p-1</annotation></semantics></math> when written in base <math id="S2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. The same argument as in the integer case gives that <math id="S2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> has no <math id="S2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-term arithmetic progression. As <math id="S2.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> also has no <math id="S2.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-term arithmetic progression. In this case, the size of <math id="S2.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-1/p)^{s}N\geq(1-1/m)^{s}N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-1/p)^{s}N\geq(1-1/m)^{s}N</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}(N):=\min_{M\leq N}\{\frac{r_{k}(M)}{M}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>M</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}(N):=\min_{M\leq N}\{\frac{r_{k}(M)}{M}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The reason for this choice is to make <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> monotone decreasing. Indeed, note that <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{k}(N)/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{k}(N)/N</annotation></semantics></math> is not monotone, as <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{r_{k}(2k-1)}{2k-1}=1-1/(2k-1)&gt;1-1/k=\frac{r_{k}(k)}{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{r_{k}(2k-1)}{2k-1}=1-1/(2k-1)&gt;1-1/k=\frac{r_{k}(k)}{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">By Bertrand’s postulate, for every integer <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a prime <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N/2\leq p\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N/2\leq p\leq N</annotation></semantics></math>. From this and Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmresult1" title="Corollary 2.1. ‣ 2 Very dense sets without long arithmetic progressions ‣ Three-color van der Waerden numbers grow super-exponentially" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, we have the following corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmresult3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmresult3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmresult3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmresult3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmresult3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmresult3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all integers <math id="S2.Thmresult3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k,t\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k,t\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmresult3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\leq(k/2)^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\leq(k/2)^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.Thmresult3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}(N)\geq(1-2/k)^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}(N)\geq(1-2/k)^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">For all <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, we get two <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-AP-free sets that together cover most of the elements <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmresult4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">

